      "writtenOn": "2022-12-09T06:37:50Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Are these methods really required ?\n\nThis dynamic interface to StateMachine is brittle and was likely originally meant to give the flexibility to add states while the machine is running (or, it\u0027s just a case of trigger-happy dynamism, which would be worse). In any case, it\u0027s never been used outside of an initialization sequence.\n\nIt sounds like you could have a *much* better interface by simply passing the list of states to the constructor, where the first state would be the initial state. This doesn\u0027t sound like a difficult diversion according to the flag (in this example, I\u0027ll assume the constructor takes a vararg SyncState) :\n\n    if (USE_SYNC_MACHINE) {\n      x \u003d new SyncState\n      y \u003d new SyncState(x)\n      z \u003d new SyncState\n      sm \u003d new SyncStateMachine(SM_NAME, x, y, z)\n    } else {\n      sm \u003d new StateMachine(SM_NAME, looper)\n      x \u003d new State\n      y \u003d new State(x)\n      z \u003d new State\n      addState(x)\n      addState(y)\n      addState(z)\n      setInitialState(x)\n    }\n\n(this can be simplified a lot if you make an interface like we discussed the other day)\n\nThis also lets you make mInitialState final, which saves you a lot of multithread worries (though mCurrentThread will still be a large problem, since it is mutable)",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 96,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 115,
        "endChar": 5
      },
      "revId": "b37e00b19eba194a98a44c49cb7aa2b5f8421728",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
